Found during k93. k93 made the native classes hold themselves weakly in their tool code; any other perl class served in-process (class entry without hub.fork) whose tools close over $self still leaks when a reload drops the entry -- the instance holds its tools, the tools hold the instance. Upstream::Perl could break it from the outside when it is dropped/stopped: empty the server's tools/prompts/resources lists, as Upstream::Fork already does for its throwaway manifest instance. Done: t/reload-free.t with a test class that closes over $self, freed after the reload.

2026-11-06 11:35: the worker for k104/k89/k75 was stopped by an API safeguard error (request req_011CgmWnQmdqQskD15HPfsSg), not by a problem in the work. No change in the repo; its partial scratch work is in scratchpad/w-hub7. Back to backlog for the maintainer.

Decision by the main session (2026-11-10), under the maintainer's 'erledige die tickets von karr'; the maintainer may veto: yes, as the card says: when Upstream::Perl is dropped or stopped it empties its server's tools/prompts/resources, as Upstream::Fork does for its manifest instance. t/reload-free.t with a test class whose tools close over $self, freed after the reload.

Correction to the decision above: only when a reload drops the entry (replaced or removed), never on an idle stop or any stop that a later call can start again from — an in-process instance that is merely stopped keeps its tools.

Committed as 79763bd (Empty an in-process class's tools, prompts and resources when a reload lets go of its entry, never on a stop).
